From b8386e4e2467d95b428d05c64323604a386b14cf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: purplewall1206 <purplewall1206@github.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 05:00:10 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] mm: introduce CortenMM skeleton: PT-page descriptors, per-PTE
 metadata, KUnit

CortenMM (SOSP'25) replaces the two-level (VMA + page table)
synchronization of memory management with a single-level abstraction:
every user page-table page ("PT page") carries a page descriptor and
memory operations run as transactions on the covering PT page.  This
commit lands the porting groundwork: the data structures, the PT-page
lifecycle hooks, and the tests.  Nothing calls into it yet -- the
protocol/transaction API (M2b) and the fault-path integration (M3)
follow in later commits, so the new code has no in-tree callers for
now.

The core objects are struct corten_ptdesc, a per-PT-page descriptor
holding the PT-page lock (rwlock_t for now), a back-link to the owning
mm, the VA window the page covers and a refcount; and struct
corten_pte_meta, an on-demand per-PTE metadata array carrying the
state that does not fit in the MMU.  Descriptors are indexed by PFN in
a global xarray.  The paper reserves one contiguous descriptor array
at boot and indexes it directly by PFN; a general-purpose kernel
cannot reserve that up front, so the xarray keeps the same O(1) PFN
lookup while allocating descriptors dynamically.  The lookup cost is
paid only on the transaction entry path, never on the fault fast path.

Design notes, documented for reviewers:

  - The per-PT-page lock starts as rwlock_t, matching the CortenMMrw
    protocol shape (paper Fig. 5).  The paper's production locks
    (BRAVO-pfqlock / MCS) are an M4+ optimization; the field is only
    reached through protocol helpers so it can be swapped without
    touching callers.

  - All descriptor and metadata allocations use GFP_NOWAIT with
    __GFP_NOWARN: the hooks run from PT-page alloc/free paths where
    sleeping or triggering reclaim could recurse into page table
    teardown.  Allocation failures are tolerated on purpose -- they
    are counted, and an untracked PT page only means "transactions on
    this page must fall back"; they never break the allocator caller.

  - The free side is covered by both PTE-page free funnels:
    ___pte_free_tlb() (TLB-batched: free_pgtables(), zap and
    PT_RECLAIM) in arch/x86/mm/pgtable.c, and pte_free() in
    include/asm-generic/pgalloc.h (synchronous: fault error handling,
    THP collapse/split, khugepaged's pte_free_defer()).  A PT page
    therefore cannot escape uninstall regardless of how it dies.
    Uninstall follows the RCU remove-before-reclaim rule: the xarray
    entry is erased first, then the base reference is dropped, and
    kfree_rcu() waits out in-flight lookups.  The allocation hook
    into pte_alloc_one() runs before the page becomes reachable
    through the page table; kernel page tables (pte_alloc_one_kernel())
    are deliberately not tracked.

Everything sits behind CONFIG_CORTEN_MM (bool, default n, depends on
MMU && X86_64) plus a static branch enabled only by the corten=on boot
parameter, so the disabled cost is one nop per PT-page alloc/free.
/sys/kernel/debug/corten/stats exposes allocation/free counters.

Six KUnit tests (CONFIG_CORTEN_MM_KUNIT_TEST) cover the metadata array
layout with full 512-entry writeback and re-initialization, the
PFN-keyed descriptor index with refcounting including double-uninstall
tolerance, and the pure covering-page selection helpers for both 4-
and 5-level paging.

Signed-off-by: purplewall1206 <purplewall1206@github.com>
